 # Hierarchical Sampling (Inverse CDF Sampling)
 
-Also called: Invers CDF, Inverse CDF, Inverse CDF Sampling, PDF Sampling, PDF Sampling via Inverse CDF, PDF sampling from weights, Sorted Piecewise Constant PDF Sampling
+Also called: Inverse CDF, Inverse CDF Sampling, PDF Sampling, PDF Sampling via Inverse CDF, PDF sampling from weights, Sorted Piecewise Constant PDF Sampling
 
 ## Overview
-Hierarchical sampling (Inverse CDF Sampling) is a method for generating importance samples along a ray in volume rendering by inverting the cumulative distribution function (CDF) of the rendering contribution [S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently placing sample points in regions of high density without redundant radiance queries, enabling differentiable end-to-end optimization of neural radiance fields [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard hierarchical sampling which samples from a piecewise constant probability density defined by coarse weights, this method uses inverse transform sampling on a piecewise approximated density field (constant or linear), allowing gradients to back-propagate through the sampling process to the density parameters [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Sampling and Jittering](2516-sampling-and-jittering.md) by providing a deterministic or stratified mechanism to concentrate fine samples where the coarse pass indicated high opacity [G].
+Hierarchical sampling (Inverse CDF Sampling) is a method for generating importance samples along a ray in volume rendering by inverting the cumulative distribution function (CDF) of the rendering contribution [S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently placing sample points in regions of high density without redundant radiance queries [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard hierarchical sampling which samples from a piecewise constant probability density defined by coarse weights, this method uses inverse transform sampling on a piecewise approximated density field (constant or linear), allowing gradients to back-propagate through the sampling process to the density parameters [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Sampling and Jittering](2516-sampling-and-jittering.md) by providing a deterministic or stratified mechanism to concentrate fine samples where the coarse pass indicated high opacity [G].
 
